1. Git Cherrypick:
    Git cherrypick is basically used to apply the specific commit changes to another branch.

    For Example:
    You are in feature branch and edit one file and push that to feature branch.
    Get the commit id using the git ---> git log --oneline
    After that checkout to main or any branch where we need to apply this specific commit and run below command.
    git cherry-pick <copied commit id from feature branch>
    git push --all (to push the chnages to remote repo)

2. Git Revert:
    Git revert is basically used to revert the last changes pushed or we revert multiple commits by keeping the commit history.

    Get the commit history using the :
    git log --oneline    (copy the commit id that we need to revert and exit the file)

    run the below command to revert the changes:
    git revert <commit id>

    Case 1:
        If you select the last commit id, it just revert the last changes by maintaining the commit history, basically it will create the new commit id.

        revert last commit only, here it create the new commit, but it revert the changes made by E commit, it still keep the changes made till D only.
        A → B → C → D → E→ E'

    Case 2:
        If you select the previoulsy committed msg , let say last 3rd commit id, in that case it will remove whatever the chnages made in that commit and it keep all the changes that we done after that commit.

        revert B only, here it create the new commit, but it revert the changes made by B commit, it still keep the changes made in D and E.
        A → B → C → D → E → B'

    Case 3:
        If you revert multiple commit like need to remove last 3 commits, then we need to revert one by one, till we needed.

        revert till B, here it create the new commit, but it revert the changes till B
        A → B → C → D → E → E' → D' → C'

3. Git Reset:
    git reset is basically used to revert the commit msg only not the changes.

    here we hv 3 types:
    a. Soft:
        git reset --soft HEAD~1
        here it removes the last commit msg and again we can push the data with git -m "new commit msg"

        Changes are already stagged we just need to push the data to repo.

    b. Mixed:
        git reset HEAD~1 or git reset --mixed
        here it removes the last commit msg and to push the data to repo, we need to run the
        git add 
        git commit -m "new commit msg"

        So basically we need to add the changes to staging then we can push.

    c. Hard:
        git reset --hard HEAD~1
        here it will removes the complete commit msg and along with that it removes the changes as well till that commit id that we passed.

        A → B → C → D
        If we reset till B, it will completely remove the changes till B commit along with that it removes the commit history as well.

    Note: Reset is generally when the changes are not pushed, if you locally committed in that case we can use, we can use this for pushed once as well, but not recomended.
